---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Code from "@/components/Code.astro";
import { SITE } from "@/lib/site";
import { withBase } from "@/lib/utils";
const snippet0 = `pip install power-pptx`;

const snippet1 = `# before
from pptx import Presentation

# after
from power_pptx import Presentation`;

const snippet2 = `from power_pptx import Presentation, BBox, audit
from power_pptx.diagrams import horizontal_pipeline

prs = Presentation()
slide = prs.slides.add_slide(prs.slide_layouts[5])   # Title Only
slide.shapes.title.text = "Pipeline overview"

horizontal_pipeline(
    slide,
    BBox.from_inches(0.5, 2.5, 9, 2.2),
    steps=["Extract", "Classify", "Enrich", "Output"],
    accent="#0B5CFF",
)

slide.shapes.add_text(
    BBox.from_inches(0.5, 5.5, 9, 1),
    text="Four-stage data pipeline.",
    align="center", size_pt=14, color="#666666",
)

slide.tidy()                  # lint + safe auto-fixes
print(audit(prs).markdown())  # optional whole-deck report
prs.save("out.pptx")`;

const snippet3 = `power-pptx lint out.pptx                  # report grouped by slide
power-pptx lint out.pptx --format json    # one JSON document for a script
power-pptx lint out.pptx --fix --output tidied.pptx   # slide.tidy(), then report`;

---

<DocsLayout
  title="Getting Started"
  description="Install power-pptx and build your first PowerPoint deck from Python in five minutes."
>
  <p class="lead">
    <code>power-pptx</code> is the actively-maintained fork of <code>python-pptx</code>. It creates,
    reads and updates PowerPoint 2007+ (<code>.pptx</code>) files on any Python-capable platform —
    no Microsoft PowerPoint required. This guide gets you from zero to a saved deck.
  </p>

  <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Python {SITE.python}, each version tested in CI</li>
    <li>
      Runtime dependencies install automatically: <code>Pillow</code> 10.1 or later,
      <code>XlsxWriter</code> 1.3.7, <code>lxml</code> 4.6.2 and <code>typing_extensions</code>
      4.9. Those minimums are the oldest releases the test suite passes on, and a CI job tests
      exactly them on Python 3.9.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
  <Code code={snippet0} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    The distribution is named <code>power-pptx</code> on PyPI but imports as
    <code>power_pptx</code>. The 2.0 release renamed the package from <code>pptx</code> so the fork
    and upstream <code>python-pptx</code> can be installed side-by-side.
  </p>

  <h3 id="optional">Optional dependencies</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Extra</th><th>Needed for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>power-pptx[svg]</code> (<code>cairosvg</code>)</td><td>Auto-rasterising the PNG fallback in <code>add_svg_picture(...)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>power-pptx[html]</code> (Playwright), then <code>python -m playwright install chromium</code></td><td>HTML capture: <code>from_html</code>, <code>add_html_figure</code>, browser-exported Plotly. <code>[embedding]</code> installs both extras</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pyyaml</code></td><td><code>DesignTokens.from_yaml(...)</code> and <code>compose.from_yaml(...)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>soffice</code> (LibreOffice) on <code>PATH</code></td><td><code>Presentation.render_thumbnails()</code> and <code>power-pptx review</code>; a <code>libreoffice</code> binary is found too</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>pdftoppm</code> (Poppler) or <code>power-pptx[review]</code> (<code>pypdfium2</code>)</td><td>Splitting the rendered PDF into one PNG per slide; without either, only one-slide decks render</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="migrating">Migrating from python-pptx</h2>
  <p>
    The fork is drop-in compatible with upstream 1.0.2. Replace <code>pptx</code> with
    <code>power_pptx</code> in your imports and everything else keeps working:
  </p>
  <Code code={snippet1} />

  <h2 id="first-deck">Your first deck</h2>
  <p>
    A minimal end-to-end pattern — open, add a slide, a diagram, some text, tidy up, save:
  </p>
  <Code code={snippet2} />

  <h2 id="check">Check a saved deck from the shell</h2>
  <p>
    The same linter runs on files, which suits a CI step that checks decks another job built.
    The exit status is <code>0</code> when nothing reaches the <code>--fail-on</code> level
    (<code>error</code> by default), <code>1</code> when something does, and <code>2</code> for a
    usage error or a file that is not a readable deck:
  </p>
  <Code code={snippet3} lang="bash" />
  <p>
    <code>--fix</code> never overwrites the input unless you also pass <code>--in-place</code>. See
    <a href={`${withBase("/advanced/lint")}#cli`}>Lint &amp; Audit</a> for SARIF output and the other
    options.
  </p>

  <h2 id="concepts">Core concepts in sixty seconds</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Presentation</strong> — the deck. <code>Presentation()</code> starts blank;
      <code>Presentation("file.pptx")</code> opens an existing one, and templates
      (<code>.potx</code>) and slide shows (<code>.ppsx</code>) open the same way. Call
      <code>prs.save("out.pptx")</code> once at the end; the extension you save under decides what
      kind of file is written.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Slides &amp; layouts</strong> — <code>prs.slides.add_slide(prs.slide_layouts[i])</code>
      appends a slide from a layout (index 5 = Title Only, 6 = Blank in the default template).
      <code>prs.slides.duplicate(slide)</code> and <code>prs.slides.remove(slide)</code> copy and
      delete slides; see <a href={withBase("/advanced/slides")}>Slides &amp; Presentations</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>BBox</strong> — an immutable rectangular region in EMU.
      <code>BBox.from_inches(x, y, w, h)</code> splats directly into every <code>add_*</code> API.
      Never write raw EMU integers — use <code>Inches</code>, <code>Pt</code>, <code>Cm</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Colours</strong> — hex strings (<code>"#0B5CFF"</code>), tuples and
      <code>RGBColor</code> all work anywhere a colour is accepted.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Space-aware authoring</strong> — the reason this fork exists. Read
      <a href={withBase("/advanced/space-aware-authoring")}>Space-Aware Authoring</a> before
      generating decks from dynamic content.
    </li>
  </ul>

